        "created_at": "2014-11-06T15:47:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>I’m not fond of saying something “should” be referred some way because of launguage “rules”. I’ve clearly touched a few nerves so allow me to unring this bell.</p>\n<p>In the most technical, prescriptive interpretation Tempest could be referred to as “he”.</p>\n<p>I’m terribly sorry if my previous statement ruffled any feathers, but I didn’t go around and say “Oh, so we should depersonalize Tempest with the term ‘it’, like any old chair, garbage can, or square of toilet paper,” or “So Tempest doesn’t get to be a singular being, but a ‘they’? Boo! Tempest doesn’t get to be a singular individual. For shame,” when others shared their perspectives.</p>\n<p>I’m assuming these terms were brought up to further information in this discussion and to provide a perspective. I provided a point I found interesting though not necessarily one I hold. Please do not assume I’m furthering the mancentric maleocracy or denying Tempest’s gender or self expression. I’m just a guy with a degree in English Literature with a tendency to splice way too many commas who had an opportunity to share something. Goodness knows I don’t get much of an opportunity when I’m brewing coffee or selling comic books.</p>\n<p>It may not be clear, but I’m quite fond of Tempest. I wouldn’t ever want to simplify how deeply interesting and complex of a being he/she/they/it/we/xhe/xhey/Tempest is.</p>",
